.TH common n 0.3 tclutils
.SH tclutils::common
.PP
Internal shared helper package for \fBtclutils\fR.
.PP
This package is not meant as the main public API, but it keeps common behavior consistent across modules.
.SS Commands
.CS
::tclutils::common::readFile path
::tclutils::common::readBinaryFile path
::tclutils::common::writeFile path data ?mode?
::tclutils::common::replaceFile path data ?-encoding utf-8? ?-translation lf?
::tclutils::common::splitLines text
::tclutils::common::splitDelimited line delimiter
::tclutils::common::parseOptions defaults ?option value ...?
.CE
.SS "File I/O"
.PP
The file helpers use Tcl 8.6 \fBtry ... finally\fR, so opened channels are closed again even if \fBread\fR, \fBwrite\fR, or later processing fails.
.PP
\fBreadBinaryFile\fR explicitly configures binary translation and uses \fBiso8859\-1\fR as byte\-preserving channel encoding. This avoids the removed Tcl 9 channel encoding name \fBbinary\fR and still preserves byte values for modules such as \fBtustrings\fR, \fBtuhexdump\fR, \fBtubase64\fR, and \fBtuzip\fR.
.PP
\fBreplaceFile\fR (0.3) writes to \fBpath.tmp<pid>\fR and renames that over \fBpath\fR. If writing fails, also when the error only shows on \fBclose\fR (a full disk), the old file stays as it was, the temporary file is removed and no channel stays open. \fB\-encoding binary\fR writes bytes. An existing file keeps its permissions (Unix). On Linux the rename is atomic; on Windows \fBfile rename \-force\fR replaces the target in two steps, but the old content is untouched until the last one.
.SS "Delimited text"
.PP
\fBsplitDelimited\fR supports both one\-character and multi\-character delimiters. It is used by \fBtucut\fR and \fBtujoin\fR, replacing duplicated private \fB_split\fR implementations.
.SS "Additional exported commands"
.PP
Documented for completeness (same module, also covered by the test suite):
.CS
common::ensureBoolean value optionName         ;# return the canonical boolean for VALUE, or throw an OPTION error naming OPTIONNAME
common::ensurePositiveInteger value what       ;# return VALUE if it is a positive integer, else throw an error naming WHAT
common::ensureOneOf value allowed what         ;# return VALUE if it is in ALLOWED, else an error listing ALLOWED (0.2)
.CE
.SS "Messages name the way out (0.2)"
.PP
An unknown option or value is reported together with what would have been accepted, so the reader does not have to open the source:
.CS
unknown option "-typ"                 unknown document type: quatsch
Known: -type -timeout                 Known: invoice delivery_note warranty
.CE
.PP
\fBparseOptions\fR does this for options, \fBensureOneOf\fR for enumerated values (errorcode \fB{TCLUTILS COMMON VALUE <what>}\fR). The errorcodes of \fBparseOptions\fR are unchanged from 0.1.
